      <h2>Fort Worth Tiny Home Builders With a Little Cowboy in Every Design</h2>
      <p>Fort Worth calls itself the city where the West begins, and it shows in what people want to build here. Clients ask for board-and-batten siding, metal roofs, deep porches and rocking-chair views — a bunkhouse on the family ranch, a casita behind a 1940s bungalow, a cabin on a few acres in Parker County. As tiny home builders in Fort Worth, Cowboy Tiny Homes pairs that western character with modern construction and layouts that make a small footprint feel generous.</p>
      <p>Not every project is rustic, of course. We design clean contemporary ADUs for infill lots near the Near Southside and the Cultural District just as readily. What every Fort Worth project shares is planning: the right home type for the land, a foundation for North Texas soil and drawings that clear the permit office.</p>

      <h2>Fort Worth Rules for ADUs and Tiny Homes</h2>
      <p>Fort Worth allows accessory dwelling units in some zoning districts, subject to conditions that can include limits on size, height, setbacks and parking, and in some cases owner-occupancy requirements. Whether a detached unit can have its own kitchen, be rented, or be built on your lot depends on your zoning district and any overlay or historic designation. The city has also discussed ways to expand housing options, so verify the current rules with Fort Worth Development Services for your address.</p>
      <p>West and south of the city, much of Parker, Johnson, Wise and Hood counties is unincorporated, where zoning typically does not apply but deed restrictions, septic rules, floodplain maps and utility availability do. That rural land is where tiny homes on wheels and park models make the most sense.</p>
      <div class="callout"><p><strong>Know before you build.</strong> Every Cowboy Tiny Homes design package starts with a feasibility study covering zoning, restrictions, easements and utilities for your property. See also our <a href="/guides/tiny-house-laws-texas/">guide to tiny house laws in Texas</a>.</p></div>

